# Cloudflare Pages response headers for STATIC ASSETS ONLY.
#
# Cloudflare does not apply this file to responses generated by Pages Functions, even when the
# request URL matches a rule (https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/), so
# `/api/solana` receives none of the headers below. `functions/api/solana.ts` sets its own —
# nosniff, no-referrer and DENY on every response it returns, refusals included — and the two must
# be kept saying the same thing.
#
# Both public pages render strings that arrive from the chain and from Arweave — that is, strings
# written by whoever signed a transaction, not by us. Escaping and shape-checking at the point of
# use is the actual fix; this file is the second wall, and it is the one that survives a future
# edit that forgets to escape something.
#
# `collect/index.html` carries its `Content-Security-Policy` as a <meta> as well, so the policy
# still applies when the file is opened from disk or when a hosting change stops applying this
# file. The <meta> copy is this file's rule minus `frame-ancestors`, which CSP3 says is ignored in
# a <meta> element; the two copies are asserted equal by `scripts/check-site.ts` in `npm test`,
# so neither can drift from what production sends.

# `Permissions-Policy` denies every page-level API the site never calls. `browsing-topics=()`
# replaces `interest-cohort=()`: FLoC was withdrawn and no browser implements that directive any
# more, so it read as protection nothing was providing. `payment`, `usb`, `hid` and `serial` are
# denied because this is a wallet page and a hardware-wallet or payment-handler prompt is
# something no page here should ever be able to raise. `web-share` is deliberately NOT listed —
# the collector's share card calls `navigator.share` (`site/collect/page.ts`),
# and the default allowlist is `self`. HSTS has no `preload` token on purpose: `.app` is
# HSTS-preloaded at the TLD level, so it would add nothing and is hard to reverse.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), browsing-topics=(), payment=(), usb=(), hid=(), serial=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

# The two static pages, by exact path rather than through a `/*` baseline.
#
# Cloudflare JOINS duplicate headers from several matching rules with a comma instead of replacing
# them (https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/), so two
# `Content-Security-Policy` headers are both enforced and the effective policy is their
# INTERSECTION: a `/*` baseline of `default-src 'none'` would combine with `/collect/*`'s
# `script-src 'self'` and forbid the collector its own bundle. Exact paths avoid that interaction
# entirely rather than relying on an untested one.
#
# Both pages are wholly static — one same-origin stylesheet (`/brand/era-shot.css`, which has no
# url() and no @import), one same-origin SVG mark, `/icon.png` as the favicon, and relative and
# mailto: links. There is no <script> and no `style=` attribute on either, so `default-src 'none'`
# covers scripts by fallback and this policy blocks nothing they use. Pages serves `index.html` at
# `/` and `privacy.html` at `/privacy`; the file paths are listed too, so a direct request for
# either is covered before any redirect.
/
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/index.html
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/privacy
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/privacy.html
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# The not-found page, when requested by its own path. A 404 answered for some other path
# carries only the /* baseline from this file plus the <meta> policy inside 404.html.
/404.html
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# The collector: same-origin bundle, same-origin RPC proxy (/api/solana) plus devnet direct, the
# two Arweave gateways for the print image, and blob: because the image bytes are hash-checked in
# JS and only then handed to <img>. Links out to explorer.solana.com are navigations, not loads.
#
# Only what the page uses. `collect/index.html` has no <style> block and no `style=` attribute,
# `collect.css` has no url() or @import, and nothing in `site/collect/` ever touches `.style` or
# `setAttribute("style", …)` — so `style-src` needs no 'unsafe-inline'. Every image is a blob:
# built from hash-checked bytes, so `img-src` needs no data:. Dropping both closes the
# CSS-attribute-selector exfiltration path and the data: image sink for any future injection.
#
# `require-trusted-types-for 'script'` turns the page's "no innerHTML anywhere" invariant
# (`site/collect/page.ts`) from a convention reviewers enforce into one the
# browser enforces: an assignment to a DOM XSS sink throws instead of executing. `trusted-types
# 'none'` allows no policy to be created, so nothing can opt back out. Neither directive falls
# back to `default-src`, so both are stated. Verified by loading the page under it.
#
# `ws://localhost:*` is Mobile Wallet Adapter's browser transport: after the `solana-wallet://`
# hand-off, Chrome on Android talks to the wallet over a WebSocket on a localhost port the wallet
# chose. Without it a Seeker collector can open the wallet but the page never hears back.
# `scripts/check-site.ts` extracts every connection origin from the built bundle and checks it here.
/collect/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self' ws://localhost:* https://api.devnet.solana.com https://turbo-gateway.com https://arweave.net; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; require-trusted-types-for 'script'; trusted-types 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# Digital Asset Links. Android reads `/.well-known/assetlinks.json` as JSON and will not accept it
# served as anything else; the short max-age keeps a fingerprint rotation from being pinned in a
# cache for a day. The file itself is generated from the release keystore — see
# `dapp-store/signing-certificate.json` — and is not committed by hand. This block labels EVERYTHING under
# `/.well-known/` as JSON, which is true today; anything else put there needs a narrower rule
# first, because `nosniff` above means a mislabelled file is refused rather than guessed at.
/.well-known/*
  Content-Type: application/json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
